<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mikhail Kalinin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mikhail Kalinin was a Bolshevik revolutionary of peasant origin who served as formal head of state of Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> from 1919 and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from 1922 until 1946. As chairman of the Central Executive Committee and later of the Presidium of the Supreme Soviet he held a largely ceremonial post, while real power lay with Stalin. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> was renamed Kaliningrad in his honour.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikhail-kalinin.jpg" alt="Mikhail Kalinin" width="330" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fyodor Kislov / TASS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Михаил_Калинин_(1940).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kalinin was born on 19 November 1875 into a peasant family in the village of Verkhnyaya Troitsa in Tver Governorate. He worked on the land with his father until he was 13, and a local landowning family later took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> as a footman. At 18 he became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lathe">lathe</a> operator at the Putilov works.</p><p>He joined the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party in 1898. He was arrested the following year, imprisoned for ten months and exiled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, where in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> he met Sergei Alliluyev and came to know Stalin through the Alliluyev family. Further arrests followed, and in 1906 he joined the Bolshevik faction and married Ekaterina Lorberg, an ethnic Estonian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At the 1912 Bolshevik conference in Prague Kalinin was elected an alternate member of the Central Committee but not a full member, because he was suspected of being an agent of the Okhrana; the real agent was Roman Malinovsky. In April 1917 he favoured conditional support for the Provisional Government, a position at odds with Lenin's. He was chosen mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> in the autumn city elections and administered it during and after the revolution of 7 November.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yakov-sverdlov">Yakov Sverdlov</a> died in March 1919, Kalinin replaced him as head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-russian-central-executive-committee">All-Russian Central Executive Committee</a>, the titular head of state. The post was renamed in 1922 and again in 1938, and he held it without a break until 1946. He became a full member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> in January 1926 and remained one until his death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The official press called Kalinin the All-Union Elder, a title that stressed his peasant origins, but by the 1930s his part in decisions was nominal. Khrushchev later said that under Stalin Kalinin was the nominal signatory of all decrees. On 5 March 1940 he was one of six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> members who signed the order to execute 25,700 Polish prisoners, which led to the Katyn massacre. His wife Ekaterina, arrested on 25 October 1938 as a Trotskyist, was sentenced in 1939 to fifteen years in a labour camp; she was released shortly before he died.</p><p>He retired in 1946 and died of cancer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 3 June that year. He was buried in the Kremlin Wall Necropolis after a state funeral. The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1945, was renamed Kaliningrad after him in 1946, and Tver was called Kalinin until its historic name was restored in 1990.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mikhail Kalinin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Formally, supreme power belonged to the All-Russian Congress of Soviets and its Central Executive Committee, chaired by Yakov Sverdlov and, from 1919, <b>Mikhail Kalinin</b>, with the Sovnarkom as the government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Verkhnaya Troitsa, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=57.25,37.15&amp;year=1921" aria-label="Verkhnaya Troitsa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mikhail Kalinin"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5330 5217h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5330 5217h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=57.25,37.15&amp;year=1921">What was here in 1921? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
